    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d472">

                <pb facs="0556" n="444" xml:id="pg444"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.01/160: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commission to Negotiate
                            Peace to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-24T11:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 24,
                                1919, 11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 3:20
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>4796. For the Secretary of State from Polk.</p>
                <p>Your telegram[s] in regard to recognition of Kolchak received. I spoke to the British as I reported but
                        Eyre Crowe<anchor n="89"
                        xml:id="d472-f1"/> apparently has no instructions. Pichon seems to be entirely in favor of it being
                    done and thinks now is the time to do so. I suggest that if you are in favor of
                    recognition of Kolchak, the United
                    States should take the lead as Great Britain and France are merely waiting for
                    an opportunity to follow. In view of all the help given Denikin by England, I think it would help our
                    prestige to take the lead in recognition. As Petrograd may be taken soon it
                    would be better to grant recognition now, if you intend ultimately to grant
                    recognition, rather than to wait.</p>
                <p>I had a conversation with Lvoff<anchor n="90"
                        xml:id="d472-f2"/> and he begged earnestly for recognition of Kolchak at the earliest possible moment. He
                    said that, in his opinion, it would help in keeping in check the reactionary
                    army people, also he stated that the Russians were having a great deal of
                    trouble with Secretary Baker in regard to
                    Russian supplies. He said that the British had done a great deal, not only for
                        Denikin but for Kolchak but apparently we had not been able to
                    deliver supplies we expected to deliver owing to the opposition of the Secretary
                    of War.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Polk</hi></signed>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Mission</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
